BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261114T000000Z
DTEND:20261114T013000Z
DTSTAMP:20261113T000000Z
SUMMARY:Ethics of Now with Lewis Akenji
DESCRIPTION:Join the Kenan Institute for Ethics and host Adriane 
 Lentz-Smith for a conversation with Lewis Akenji on reorienting the economy 
 to fight climate change and promote human wellbeing.\n\nWhether pushing 
 environments to new extremes\, fracturing local economies\, or heightening 
 social tensions\, climate change is impacting us all — but the way we 
 talk about it is not leading us towards solutions. Lewis Akenji says we 
 tend to focus on consumerist lifestyles rather than the economic structures 
 that drive these behaviors in the first place — and the kind of society 
 we want to build instead.\n\nJoin Akenji to explore how we can regain our 
 agency around climate change by acting as collectives instead of 
 individuals\, and how\, as communities and governments\, we can address the 
 interlinked crises of climate change and economic insecurity in ways that 
 promote human wellbeing.\n\nAdditional biographical information:\n\nLewis 
 Akenji is a political economist with an interest in institutions\, 
 policies\, and the allocation of scarce resources towards competing 
 societal objectives within ecological limits. He is Executive Director of 
 the Berlin-based think tank Hot or Cool Institute\, and co-chair of the 
 Club of Rome Taskforce on Materials and Consumption. He is currently 
 Executive-in-Residence at the Duke Initiative for Science & Society.\n\nHe 
 has consulted with organizations including United Nations agencies\, Asian 
 and African development banks\, the European Commission\, the Association 
 of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN)\, and as technical or science-policy 
 adviser to several national government delegations. Lewis has an M.Sc. 
 Sustainable Resource Management (Technical University Munich\, Germany) and 
 a Ph.D. Political Economy (University of Helsinki\, Finland).\n\nAdditional 
 event information:\n\nThis event takes place at the Durham County Main 
 Library. Parking is available onsite. If you have any questions related to 
 accessibility needs\, please contact Hillary Train at 
 hillary.train@duke.edu. A reception will follow the event.\n\nCosponsors 
 for this event included Durham County Library\, the Duke Initiative in 
 Science and Society\, and the Sanford School of Public Policy at Duke 
 University.
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Larkin Coffey":MAILTO:lcoffey@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Author Visit, Humanities
UID:LibCal-14288-16756508
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16756508
END:VEVENT
