BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261117T220000Z
DTEND:20261118T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T000000Z
SUMMARY:Theory of Change - Film Screening
DESCRIPTION:What happens when working-class people stop waiting for change 
 and start building it?\n\nTheory of Change follows three working-class 
 mothers in rural North Carolina as they organize their neighbors\, build 
 local power\, and discover what’s possible when communities come together 
 to create change.\n\nThe screening is part of a statewide effort to bring 
 Theory of Change into communities across North Carolina—creating spaces 
 for people to come together\, share their stories\, talk about the issues 
 affecting their families\, and connect with others ready to build power 
 locally.\n\nAfter the film\, we’ll gather for a facilitated conversation 
 about the story\, the challenges facing working-class communities\, and 
 what it looks like to turn connection into action.\n\n\nWhether you're 
 already involved in your community or simply curious about what's possible 
 when neighbors come together\, we'd love to have you there.\n\nFree and 
 open to the public. Registration required.\n\nCome watch the film. Stay for 
 the conversation. Find your people. Build power.
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Austin Daniels":MAILTO:ausdaniels@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Business, Community Engagement, Movie
UID:LibCal-14288-17747756
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17747756
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261117T230000Z
DTEND:20261118T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20261117T000000Z
SUMMARY:Write it Down: Letters\, Archives\, and Creative Response
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an evening of letter reading and letter writing 
 inspired by archival materials from the Rosetta Reitz papers at Duke 
 University. The program will be guided by singer/songwriter and Duke 
 Practitioner in Residence Tift Merritt and Duke Senior Research Scholar Lou 
 Brown\, co-leaders of an ongoing research project into the processes and 
 practices of this feminist cultural entrepreneur. They will share a couple 
 of letters that have inspired their own imagination and create space for 
 everyone to read and write letters of their own. Bring a letter from your 
 own archives to share if you would like!\n\nAll are 
 welcome!\n\nRegistration required at the link below.\n\nNOTE: WHILE THIS 
 PROGRAM WILL BE HELD IN THE MAIN CHILDREN’S PROGRAMMING ROOM AT THE 
 DURHAM COUNTY MAIN LIBRARY\, THE BOOK IS MOST APPROPRIATE FOR OLDER TEENS 
 AND ADULTS.\n\nThis program is part of “Forum @ the Library” – a 
 monthly series featuring Duke researchers and faculty sharing their work in 
 an interactive format\, hosted by the Durham County Library (DCL) in 
 partnership with the Forum for Scholars and Publics at the Franklin 
 Humanities Institute. For this event\, we also acknowledge the support of 
 Bass Connections at Duke University.
LOCATION:Main Children's Programming Room\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Larkin Coffey":MAILTO:lcoffey@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Author Visit, Music
UID:LibCal-14288-16625687
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16625687
END:VEVENT
