BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261118T230000Z
DTEND:20261119T003000Z
DTSTAMP:20261118T000000Z
SUMMARY:COYOTES!
DESCRIPTION:COYOTES! \n\nWho? What? Where? When? Why? \n\n \n\n\n\nArriving 
 in the 1980s and now found in all 100 counties in North Carolina... 
 \n\nTheir name translates to the “barking dog” and they are also known 
 as the "song dog" due to having one of the largest ranges of vocalizations 
 of North American mammals... \n\nFilling the ecological niche previously 
 occupied by wolves and cougars... \n\nRevered by indigenous people for 
 their intelligence and adaptability...reviled by Anglo colonizers as 
 cowardly and untrustworthy...hundreds of thousands are killed each year 
 across the country... \n\nColorblind and seeing only in shades of blue and 
 yellow just like their domestic and wild relatives the dog and wolf...\n\n 
 \n\n\n\nWho is the most adaptable mammal in North America? What do coyotes 
 eat? Where can you see coyotes in Durham? When do coyote packs begin to 
 disperse from the current years litter? Why are coyotes more nocturnal in 
 urban areas?  \n\n \n\n\n\nJoin Falyn Owens\, North Carolina Wildlife 
 Resources Commission Wildlife Extension Biologist\, to learn more about the 
 Wile E. Coyote!  \n\n  \n\nFalyn Owens is the Wildlife Extension Biologist 
 with the North Carolina Wildlife Resources Commission. She works with 
 organizations\, businesses\, and communities to find ways for people and 
 wildlife to coexist in our shared world. Her main goal is helping people 
 understand the wild species that live all around us––from birds and 
 beavers to coyotes and bears––and use that knowledge to prevent 
 problems or address them responsibly if they do occur. You might have seen 
 her on the local news talking about snakes\, deer\, or other common 
 species. She led the NC Fox and Coyote Management Advisory Council and 
 regularly teaches workshops about these ubiquitous canids. Falyn also 
 trains wildlife control professionals and rehabilitators on handling 
 wildlife encounters safely\, humanely\, and legally. Outside of work\, she 
 enjoys hiking\, foraging for mushrooms\, and learning about all the smaller 
 critters and plants that people tend not to notice.  \n\n  \n\nWednesday 
 November 18th starting at 6 p.m. in the Durham County Main Library 
 auditorium located at 300 North Roxboro Street in Durham.     \n\nParking 
 is available in the library parking lot or at the North Queen Street Garage 
 located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind the apartments directly 
 across the street from the library. Parking at the garage is free. Please 
 bring your parking ticket to a service point for validation.       
 \n\nThere will be no live animals at this event.   \n\nRegistration is 
 appreciated to receive an email reminder\, cancellation link\, and program 
 updates. Seating is first come first served. Registration does not hold or 
 guarantee a seat.     \n\n   \n\nQuestions? Email mkoslofsky@dconc.gov 
 \n\n\n\nNote: If you have any accessibility questions or accommodation 
 needs\, please contact me at mkoslofsky@dconc.gov or call 919-560-9311. 
 Please make all requests three business days prior to the event to ensure I 
 can provide the best accommodations possible! \n\n\n\nImage description: A 
 coyote standing on a lakeshore displaying the typical coat pattern of red\, 
 orange\, brown\, and white.  \n\nImage credit: USFWS Pacific Southwest 
 Region
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Megan Koslofsky":MAILTO:mkoslofsky@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Durham, Environmental, STEAM
UID:LibCal-14288-15433377
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/15433377
END:VEVENT
