BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261119T203000Z
DTEND:20261119T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20261119T000000Z
SUMMARY:Croaks and Leaps: A Frog Finding Experience
DESCRIPTION:What's a frog and how do we find them? Join researchers Mary 
 Diez and Joel Mota from NC State and learn all about them! We will start 
 things off with a brief overview of amphibians and frogs in North Carolina. 
 Then\, we'll apply what we learned and do a hands-on group activity where 
 we will count frogs in some "swamps"! Afterwards\, we'll have a group 
 debrief and talk a little bit more about our scientist's research and the 
 tools they use in the field to count frogs. Plus\, there may be a real frog 
 or two to gaze upon... but you won't know unless you attend! It will be a 
 ribbiting good time you won't want to miss! \n\nWhile the activity will be 
 aimed for a younger audience\, all ages are welcome to come learn about and 
 celebrate frogs!\n\nRegistration is required\, please register everyone in 
 your party. \n\n \n\nMary Diez\, a North Carolina native\, joined North 
 Carolina State University’s Department of Applied Ecology and NC 
 Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit as a Master’s student in 
 Spring of 2023. Working under Jaime Collazo and Nathan Hostetter she 
 graduated with her Master of Science in Biology in the Fall of 2025 after 
 having spent the last couple of years counting tiny frogs in a swamp in 
 Puerto Rico. She is currently working as a research associate under Nathan 
 Hostetter in the NC Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit where along 
 with planning out future coquí frog projects\, she is expanding her 
 quantitative ecologist skills with Canada Lynx occupancy modeling. Mary 
 currently lives in Raleigh\, NC and enjoys biking\, reading\, making 
 things\, and looking for critters.\n\nJoel Mota grew up as an immigrant in 
 the sprawling suburbs of Northern Virginia. He was born in Portugal\, where 
 his passion for herpetology was sparked during childhood summers visiting 
 family where he would dart amongst sandy pinewoods capturing little 
 lizards. He graduated with a B.Sc. in Biology\, with a minor in chemistry 
 from George Mason University. With his bachelors in hand\, he focused on 
 acquiring wildlife and ecological research experience and hopped from one 
 internship/technician position to another\, taking him throughout the 
 southern Mid-Atlantic and Southeastern United States. He also traveled to 
 Myanmar to assist on Asian elephant research being conducted by the 
 Smithsonian Conservation Biology Institute and worked on projects that 
 directly affected conservation decisions in Malaysia and Chad. Joel took 
 the next step and completed a Master of Science in Wildlife and Fisheries 
 Resources at West Virginia University. His work focused on the conservation 
 of spotted turtles in West Virginia. Now he has been at North Carolina 
 State University conducting his PhD work\, which focuses on how timber 
 management practices affect herpetological communities. In his spare time 
 Joel enjoys cooking\, baking\, cycling\, and nurturing a nascent gardening 
 hobby. He has enjoyed spending time in the different landscapes North 
 Carolina has to offer\; from swimming in the Atlantic Ocean looking for 
 crabs and endangered butterflies to hiking in the mountains and being 
 overwhelmed by the great diversity of salamanders.
LOCATION:Southwest Meeting Room\, Southwest Regional Library (3605 Shannon Rd)
ORGANIZER;CN="Raivynn Smith":MAILTO:raivynnsmith@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Accommodations Available, Classes & Lectures, Durham, Environmental, STEAM
UID:LibCal-14288-17267196
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17267196
END:VEVENT
